{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-142395","model_call":7,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ teen teenager \"high school student\") (+ \"animal testing\" \"animal tests\") (+ alternative alternatives replacement))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ teen teenager \"high school student\") (+ \"animal testing\" \"animal tests\") (+ alternative alternatives replacement))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1424322634#7_3216336157","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q991","rank":1,"snippet":"would require a revolution in testing methods, equipment, lab facilities, scientists, and, of course, attitudes. Listening to Marenus, it’s clear that China’s regulatory process can be slower than in the United States. “ A product has to go through three agencies, who review each application,” he says. It can take six months to over a year for a new product to be reviewed by all the agencies, and Marenus describes the regulatory bodies’ attitudes as a “checkbox mentality,” not allowing for much wiggle room or debate. How MAC Thinks They Can Help By being part of the system, MAC believes they’re changing it from within. In 2012, IIVS created the Industry Council for the Advancement for the Regulatory Acceptance of <cover>Alternatives (ICARAA). This group — comprising several major corporations, including Estée Lauder Companies — focuses on lectures, lab demos, hands-on training, and data interpretation to help move toward non-animal testing.\" , \"start_char\": 3958.000000 , \"title\": \"MAC Is Finally Speaking Out Amid Criticism for Animal Testing | Teen </cover>Vogue\" , \"url\": \"https://www.teenvogue.com/story/mac-animal-testing-china-criticism\" }"},"time":332},"model_calls":7,"document_judgments":3,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
