{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-142395","model_call":51,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ students teens \"high school\") (+ dissection vivisection) (+ \"student choice\" petition campaign policy))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ students teens \"high school\") (+ dissection vivisection) (+ \"student choice\" petition campaign policy))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_555980527#10_982777753","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1029","rank":1,"snippet":"used for dissection: Sources of dissected animals: What happens to the animals used for dissection? The effect on the environment: Types of classes in which dissection is taught: The danger to students Objecting to dissection Alternatives to dissection: The expense States that have informed student consent laws (K-12): States with Student Choice Policies: States with legislation proposing Student Choice: Passing a Student Choice Policy: \" , \"segment\": \"Talk to other students and go to the professor or department head as a group to explain your objection to dissection. Create a <cover>petition against dissection in your school and ask other students </cover>to sign it. Give copies to the department head, principal, and anyone else you think could help. Ask your parent (s) or guardian (s) to write a letter in support of your decision. If the department head or dean is similarly unresponsive, contact the national Dissection Hotline at (800) 922-FROG or AnimaLearn at (800) 729-2287. Use your campus newspaper and radio station to begin a dialogue about dissection alternatives at your school. Feel free to contact an animal protection organization at any point; we are here to help! Alternatives to dissection: Numerous studies ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_555980527#10_982777753","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":1856},"model_calls":51,"document_judgments":34,"relevance_3":2,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_cal